<%#
  T-26a increment 3 — THE OWNED MY-PAGE ISSUE REPORT WIDGET (FR-01).

  This file used to render one of the BASE PLUGIN's report templates through
  `my/_report.erb`, which iframed that plugin's own route. Nothing here does any more: the
  lookup is `WidgetReport` (`Template.visible(actor)`) and the render is
  `Reporting::ReportRun`. With this, no surface of this plugin needs the base plugin.

  --- THIS FILE'S PATH IS ITS REGISTRATION, AND THAT IS WHY THE GUARD IS HERE ---

  Redmine core discovers my-page blocks by GLOBBING plugin view directories —
  `Dir.glob(".../*/app/views/my/blocks/_*.{rhtml,erb}")` in `lib/redmine/my_page.rb`,
  unchanged 5.1 -> 7.0 — so the existence of this file makes this plugin contribute a core
  my-page block on every install, with no line in `init.rb` anywhere. Then note what core
  does NOT do: `MyHelper#render_block_content` rescues only `ActionView::MissingTemplate`,
  so ANY other exception from this body is a 500 on `/my/page` — taking down the one page
  that carries this block's own close button. That is §Findings **E-39**, measured.

  So the body is wrapped, and the rescue takes `ScriptError` as well as `StandardError`
  (a class body that fails to parse raises `SyntaxError`; a bad `require` raises
  `LoadError`; neither is a `StandardError`). It logs and degrades VISIBLY rather than
  swallowing — CLAUDE.md §5.

  THE PLACEHOLDER IS DELIBERATELY NOT NIL. `MyHelper#render_block` wraps a block only
  `if content.present?`, so a widget that renders as nothing loses the contextual controls
  that carry its own close button, and the user is left with a block they cannot remove.

  Nothing is appended to the output buffer before the rescue: the only writes in the
  guarded body are the two `render` calls, and `render` builds its result before
  returning, so a raise inside one leaves the buffer untouched.

  (Written without its ERB delimiters on purpose, and neither delimiter appears anywhere in
  this comment for the same reason. ERB ends a comment at the first closing delimiter it
  meets, so quoting either one inside a comment ends it there and prints everything after
  it — which is what this file did on /my/page, for thirty lines, for every user who added
  the block. Findings M-4; script/gates/erb_comment_integrity.sh is what stops the next
  one, and it checks for BOTH delimiters because the first version of this very sentence
  reintroduced the defect with the closing one.)

  --- WHAT IS DIFFERENT FROM THE PROJECT DASHBOARD ---

  `include_all_helpers = false`, so this view cannot call this plugin's helpers and neither
  can a partial it renders (a partial runs in the CALLING controller's view context). Every
  piece of presentation it needs is therefore a MODULE — `ReportFrame`, `DegradationText` —
  which is why those two were extracted rather than left as helper methods.

  There is no project in the URL, and that is the whole of the difference. Until T-52 there
  was no project SELECTOR either, on the argument that the saved query defines the input and
  a second way of saying it is a second thing that can disagree. That argument was measured
  and did not survive: a query with no project of its own counts every project the viewer
  can see, siblings included, and nothing in the settings could say otherwise. The settings
  now carry a project, blank by default, and it bounds the ROWS only —
  `WidgetReport.render_for_my_page` argues both halves.
%>
<% begin %>
  <%
    block ||= 'report_by_issues'
    # `User.current` is read ONCE, here, and passed explicitly from then on (INV-1). A
    # my-page block genuinely is "the page of whoever is logged in" — this is the one place
    # that fact enters, rather than being re-read three frames down.
    rrd_actor = User.current
    settings ||= rrd_actor.pref.my_page_settings(block)
    rrd_widget = RedmineReporterDashboards::WidgetReport.render_for_my_page(
      actor: rrd_actor, block: block, settings: settings, logger: Rails.logger
    )
  %>

  <%# T-52 — THE THIRD STATE, AND THE SETTINGS FORM RENDERS UNDER IT.
      A stored project this actor may no longer read shows NO figures: the owner asked for
      one project, and a report that silently grew because a permission was withdrawn is the
      wrong way to be wrong (INV-3, CLAUDE.md §4). It is not an error placeholder either —
      nothing went wrong, the setting is simply unusable.
      The FORM is what makes it a state rather than a dead end. Core gives a my-page block
      only Move and Delete, and this partial's unresolved branch is the only place the
      settings form exists at all — so a bare notice would tell the reader to change a
      setting that was no longer on the page. Measured by an independent review: deleting
      and re-adding the block was the only way out, and
      `UserPreference#clear_unused_block_settings` throws the template and query away with
      it. %>
  <% if rrd_widget && !rrd_widget.project_unavailable %>
    <%= render partial: 'reporter_dashboards/widgets/my_report',
               locals: { block: block, widget: rrd_widget, user: rrd_actor } %>
  <% else %>
    <% if rrd_widget %>
      <p class="nodata"><%= l(:text_reporter_widget_project_unavailable) %></p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render partial: 'reporter_dashboards/widgets/my_report_settings',
               locals: { block: block, settings: settings, report_type: :issues,
                         queries: IssueQuery.visible(rrd_actor).sorted,
                         report_templates: RedmineReporterDashboards::WidgetReport.templates_for(
                           project: nil, actor: rrd_actor, source: 'issues'
                         ),
                         projects: RedmineReporterDashboards::WidgetReport.projects_for(
                           actor: rrd_actor
                         ) } %>
  <% end %>
<% rescue StandardError, ScriptError => rrd_error %>
  <% RedmineReporterDashboards::WidgetReport.log_my_page_failure(rrd_error) %>
  <p class="nodata"><%= l(:error_reporter_widget_render_failed) %></p>
<% end %>
